<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<databaseChangeLog
        xmlns="http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog"
        xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
        xsi:schemaLocation="
           http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog
           http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog/dbchangelog-latest.xsd">

    <!--
      strategy_defaults.target_mode / sl_mode: which of the two bracket columns
      a strategy actually exits on.

      The problem
      ===========
      Since 027 a config carries BOTH shapes of every bracket side - the absolute
      trade_config.target / stop_loss points and the premium-relative target_pct /
      sl_pct - and OrderService.bracketAtEntry picked between them by a rule that
      was never written down as a setting:

          pct != null && pct > 0  ->  entryPrice * pct
          otherwise               ->  the absolute column

      i.e. "a percentage, if one happens to be set, silently wins". The points
      column stays populated and visible in the admin UI while being dead, so
      editing it changes nothing and nothing says why. Every one of the 235
      standing configs is in exactly that state: target = 20.00 alongside
      target_pct = 0.2000, and only the 0.2000 has ever decided an exit.

      Turning the implicit rule into a column is the whole point of this
      changeset. "Which bracket this strategy exits on" decides when a trade
      closes, so per CLAUDE.md #9 it is config, not an if-statement.

      Why strategy_defaults and not trade_config
      ==========================================
      The unit of the decision is the strategy. A trade_config can name several
      strategies (035's strategy_ids) and TradeConfigScheduler fans it out into
      one TradeConfigCombinedDTO per id, so "config 2652 under strategy 1 exits
      on points, under strategy 3 on percentage" is expressible only if the mode
      is keyed by strategy. It is also the table 033/040 already established for
      exactly this class of per-strategy trading decision.

      Two columns, not one
      ====================
      OrderService resolves target and stop-loss independently and 036's bracket
      is deliberately asymmetric - cap the stop, let the target run - so a single
      mode could not express the mixed bracket (points target, percentage stop)
      that this split makes reachable.

      Why these do NOT join configSignature()
      ======================================
      Deliberate divergence from 040, which added opposite_side to the signature.
      A generated trade_config carries BOTH the points and the percentage columns
      already, and the mode is resolved at order time from the DTO's strategyId -
      it is never stamped onto the config row. So two strategies that differ only
      in bracket mode can share one generated config and still exit differently,
      and splitting the group would emit a duplicate row that changes nothing.
      opposite_side had to join the signature because it changes what the
      generated row *contains* (which leg it trades); these do not.

      Default PERCENT is behaviour-neutral
      ====================================
      PERCENT reproduces the legacy rule exactly: prefer the pct, fall back to the
      absolute column when it is unset. So every existing row - and any strategy
      with no strategy_defaults row at all, which BracketMode.parse(null) also
      reads as PERCENT - keeps the bracket it has today, and the S6 sign-off
      ("the percentage bracket is live and staying live") stands untouched.
      Switching a strategy to POINTS is a Rule 0 behaviour change and needs its
      own measured before/after pair on the same window.
    -->
    <changeSet id="041_add_bracket_mode_to_strategy_defaults" author="moneymaker">
        <!--
          Guarded for the reason 035 spelled out: this project runs
          spring.jpa.hibernate.ddl-auto=update. Liquibase normally wins the race
          (Spring Boot runs it before the EntityManagerFactory is built), but a
          branch booted with the entity present and this file not yet in the
          master leaves Hibernate to create the columns, and a bare addColumn
          then dies on next start with "Duplicate column name". MARK_RAN keeps
          that database startable; the seed changeset below still fills the
          values either way.
        -->
        <preConditions onFail="MARK_RAN">
            <not>
                <columnExists tableName="strategy_defaults" columnName="target_mode"/>
            </not>
        </preConditions>
        <addColumn tableName="strategy_defaults">
            <column name="target_mode" type="VARCHAR(8)" defaultValue="PERCENT">
                <constraints nullable="false"/>
            </column>
            <column name="sl_mode" type="VARCHAR(8)" defaultValue="PERCENT">
                <constraints nullable="false"/>
            </column>
        </addColumn>
    </changeSet>

    <!--
      addColumn's default covers rows inserted from here on and MySQL backfills
      existing rows with it, but 036 established the pattern of making that
      guarantee explicit rather than engine-dependent. Scoped to NULL so a re-run
      cannot overwrite a mode someone has since switched.
    -->
    <changeSet id="041_seed_bracket_mode_defaults" author="moneymaker">
        <update tableName="strategy_defaults">
            <column name="target_mode" value="PERCENT"/>
            <where>target_mode IS NULL</where>
        </update>
        <update tableName="strategy_defaults">
            <column name="sl_mode" value="PERCENT"/>
            <where>sl_mode IS NULL</where>
        </update>
    </changeSet>

</databaseChangeLog>
